<ContentLayout title="Clamp-On LED Desk Lamp vs BenQ ScreenBar: Which Desk Light Should You Buy? | StackGeist" description="A $25 clamp lamp lights the whole desk. A $130 monitor bar lights the desk without eating space. Here is when each answer is correct." active="gear">
  <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">StackGeist</a> / <a href="/gear">Gear</a> / <a href="/gear/budget-tech">Budget tech</a> / <a href="/gear/budget-tech/compare">Compare</a> / Clamp LED lamp vs BenQ ScreenBar</p>
  <p class="page-kicker">Head to head</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">Clamp-On LED Desk Lamp vs BenQ ScreenBar</h1>
  <p class="page-lead">Both are task lighting for a desk. The clamp lamp is a versatile flexible-arm light that puts brightness exactly where you need it, at a fraction of the price. The BenQ ScreenBar clips to the top of your monitor, uses zero desk space, and has an asymmetric optical design that avoids screen glare. Pick based on desk footprint and how much you value engineering polish.</p>

  <section class="content-section">
    <table class="cmp">
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Clamp-On LED Desk Lamp</th><th>BenQ ScreenBar Pro</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody><tr><td>Price</td><td>$20-30</td><td>$110-140</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mounting</td><td>Clamp to desk edge</td><td>Counterweight on monitor top</td></tr>
<tr><td>Desk footprint</td><td>Small clamp base</td><td>None</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brightness levels</td><td>3-5 typically</td><td>Continuous dimming</td></tr>
<tr><td>Color temperature</td><td>3000K-6500K adjustable</td><td>2700K-6500K adjustable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Auto brightness sensor</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (ScreenBar Halo has motion + ambient)</td></tr>
<tr><td>CRI (color accuracy)</td><td>80+ typical</td><td>95+</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power source</td><td>USB or wall wart</td><td>USB from monitor / laptop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Broad task lighting on any surface</td><td>Screen work with no desk clutter</td></tr></tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When Clamp-On LED Desk Lamp wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick Clamp-On LED Desk Lamp if...</h2>
    <p>You want the maximum light per dollar. A $25 clamp lamp with adjustable brightness and color temperature covers 90% of what a $130 ScreenBar does at 20% of the price.</p><p>You need light beyond the monitor area — sketching, reading physical documents, hobby work on the side of the desk. The flexible arm points wherever you need. The ScreenBar only lights below the monitor.</p><p>Your monitor is unusual (ultra-wide beyond ScreenBar compatibility, curved past 1500R, VESA-mounted without clearance on top). The clamp lamp doesn't care about monitor shape.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">When BenQ ScreenBar Pro wins</p>
    <h2 class="section-heading">Pick BenQ ScreenBar Pro if...</h2>
    <p>You want zero desk clutter. The ScreenBar clips to the top of the monitor and uses zero desk surface. On a small desk this is the reason to spend the money.</p><p>You do color-critical work (photo editing, graphic design). The ScreenBar's CRI 95+ is genuinely important for judging color; cheap LED lamps at CRI 80 can shift how skin tones and product colors appear.</p><p>You care about screen glare. The ScreenBar's asymmetric lens design is engineered to avoid bouncing light off the screen. Cheap desk lamps aimed near a monitor produce visible reflections.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <p class="page-kicker">The verdict</p>
    <p>Small desk, screen-focused work, willing to spend $130: BenQ ScreenBar. Any other case: buy the clamp lamp and pocket the $100.</p><p>The ScreenBar is a good product. It's just not a $100-better product than a decent clamp lamp for most people.</p>
  </section>

